Experiment 9: Process Creation and Termination Using C
Model Institute of Engineering & Technology (Autonomous) | Course: COM-312 Operating System Lab
1. Aim & Objectives
Aim: To write and execute a C program that creates a child process, waits for its termination, and displays its Process ID (PID) under Linux.
Step 1: Explain what a Process ID (PID) is and how to retrieve it using getpid().
Step 2: Demonstrate process cloning using fork() and observe dual return values.
Step 3: Synchronize parent and child processes using wait(NULL) and display the terminated child's PID.
2. Step 1: What is a Process ID (PID)?
Whenever any C program runs, the operating system assigns it a unique identification number—similar to a student roll number. This is called the Process ID (PID). We use the getpid() function from <unistd.h> to check it.
Source Code: Filename: step1_pid.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    // getpid() returns the PID of the currently running process
    printf("I am a simple process. My PID is: %d\n", getpid());
    return 0;
}


Key Teaching Takeaway: One running program equals one active process with one unique PID.
Compilation and Output:
$ gcc step1_pid.c -o step1_pid
$ ./step1_pid
I am a simple process. My PID is: 3412


3. Step 2: Creating a Child Process with fork()
Calling fork() clones the running program into two separate, concurrent processes:
1. The Child process, where fork() returns 0.
2. The Parent process, where fork() returns the child's positive PID.
Source Code: Filename: step2_fork.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    pid_t p = fork();

    // From this point onward, both processes execute the same code
    if (p == 0) {
        // Only the child executes this branch
        printf("I am the CHILD process! My own PID: %d\n", getpid());
    } else {
        // Only the parent executes this branch
        printf("I am the PARENT process! fork() gave me child's PID: %d\n", p);
    }

    return 0;
}


Observation for Students: Both processes run independently. Sometimes the parent finishes first, and sometimes the child finishes first, leading to interleaved terminal output.
Compilation and Output:
$ gcc step2_fork.c -o step2_fork
$ ./step2_fork
I am the PARENT process! fork() gave me child's PID: 3450
I am the CHILD process! My own PID: 3450


4. Step 3: Synchronizing with wait() to Display the Child's PID
To ensure orderly execution, we introduce wait() from <sys/wait.h>. The parent process halts its execution until the child finishes working.
Calling wait(NULL) serves two crucial purposes:
1. It pauses (blocks) the parent process until the child exits.
2. It returns the PID of the child process that just terminated.
Main Lab Source Code: Filename: process_creation_wait.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main() {
    pid_t p = fork();

    if (p == 0) {
        // --- CHILD BRANCH ---
        printf("[Child] Work started. My PID: %d\n", getpid());
        sleep(2); // Pause so students can clearly observe the parent waiting
        printf("[Child] Work finished! Exiting now.\n");
        exit(0);
    } 
    else {
        // --- PARENT BRANCH ---
        printf("[Parent] Child started. Now waiting for it to finish...\n");

        // wait(NULL) halts the parent and returns the terminated child's PID
        pid_t terminated_pid = wait(NULL);

        printf("[Parent] Child with PID %d has terminated!\n", terminated_pid);
    }

    return 0;
}


Compilation and Output:
$ gcc process_creation_wait.c -o exp9
$ ./exp9
[Parent] Child started. Now waiting for it to finish...
[Child] Work started. My PID: 3450
[Child] Work finished! Exiting now.
[Parent] Child with PID 3450 has terminated!


5. Line-by-Line Code Breakdown
Code Statement
Operating System Explanation
pid_t p = fork();
Clones calling process into two execution paths. Returns 0 to child and child's PID to parent.
if (p == 0)
Evaluates true only inside the child process. The child enters this block.
printf("...PID: %d", getpid());
getpid() queries the kernel and prints the process's own unique PID.
sleep(2);
Pauses child for 2 seconds so students clearly observe the parent actively waiting.
exit(0);
Terminates the child cleanly so it does not execute parent statements.
else { ... }
Evaluates true inside the parent process, where p contains the positive child PID.
pid_t term_pid = wait(NULL);
Blocks parent until child exits, then harvests and returns the terminated child's PID.


6. Quick Summary for the Whiteboard
fork(): Clones the calling process. Returns 0 to the child and the child's PID to the parent.
getpid(): Retrieves the current process's own PID.
wait(NULL): Pauses the parent and returns the PID of the child once it exits.
